150 <class 'list'>
L07-09 cards 45 E1-c092 E1-c151
E1-c092 L07 T | How are consumer surplus, producer surplus and total surplus defined for a volume Q? || All trade is at one fee p = P(Q). Consumer surplus is CS(Q) = integral from 0 to Q of P(x) dx minus pQ. Producer surplus is PS(Q) = (p - c) Q, since there is no fixed cost. Total surplus is W(Q) = CS(Q) + PS(Q) (Definition 1.1). || basis= None

E1-c093 L07 T | Under linear demand, what are CS, PS and total surplus W as functions of Q? || Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), and for 0 <= Q <= a: CS(Q) = Q^2/(2b), PS(Q) = (d - Q)Q/b, and W(Q) = (2dQ - Q^2)/(2b), which equals the integral from 0 to Q of (P(x) - c) dx (Theorem 1.2). || basis= None

E1-c094 L07 None | What is the deadweight loss at volume Q? || DWL(Q) = W(d) - W(Q), the shortfall of total surplus below its competitive level, where the competitive benchmark has volume d (Definition 1.3). || basis= None

E1-c095 L07 T | Which volume maximizes total surplus? || Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), the competitive volume Q = d maximizes W on 0 <= Q <= a, where W(d) = d^2/(2b) (Lemma 1.4). || basis= None

E1-c096 L07 T | What is the deadweight loss under linear demand? || Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), DWL(Q) = (d - Q)^2/(2b) for 0 <= Q <= a. It is zero only at Q = d and positive for every other volume in [0, a] (Theorem 1.5). || basis= None

E1-c097 L07 T | What are the surplus values of the monopoly outcome (volume d/2)? || Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), CS = d^2/(8b), PS = d^2/(4b), W = 3d^2/(8b) and DWL = d^2/(8b) (Corollary 2.1). || basis= None

E1-c098 L07 T | What are the surplus values of the Cournot outcome (volume 2d/3)? || Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), CS = 2d^2/(9b), PS = 2d^2/(9b), W = 4d^2/(9b) and DWL = d^2/(18b) (Corollary 2.1). || basis= None

E1-c099 L07 T | What are the surplus values of the Bertrand outcome (volume d)? || Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), CS = d^2/(2b), PS = 0, W = d^2/(2b) and DWL = 0 (Corollary 2.1). || basis= None

E1-c100 L07 T | For (a, b, c) = (120, 1, 20), what are the surplus values in cents? || The competitive total surplus is 5000 cents. The monopoly has CS 1250, PS 2500, W 3750 and DWL 1250 cents. The Cournot DWL is 5000/9, which is about 555.56 and is 556 cents to the nearest cent, half up (Example 2.2). || basis= None

E1-c101 L07 T | How do the three structures order in losses, consumer surplus, producer surplus and total surplus? || Under the hypotheses of Definition 1.1 (Lesson 6): 0 < DWL_C < DWL_M and DWL_B = 0. CS_M < CS_C < CS_B, PS_M > PS_C > PS_B = 0 and W_M < W_C < W_B. The monopoly loss is one quarter of the competitive total surplus d^2/(2b), and the Cournot loss is one ninth of it (Proposition 2.3). || basis= None

E1-c102 L07 T | Is the monopoly's profit the deadweight loss? || No. Profit is a transfer from consumers, and it counts in total surplus. The loss is W(d) - W(Q), which is 1250 cents for the reference monopoly, while the monopoly profit is 2500 cents (the misconception that the monopoly profit is the deadweight loss; Section 4). || basis= None

E1-c103 L07 T | With two exchanges, is there no deadweight loss? || No. Under the hypotheses of Lesson 6, Definition 1.1 (b > 0, c > 0, a > bc), Cournot competition has DWL = d^2/(18b) > 0. Only the Bertrand outcome has zero loss, because its volume is d (Corollary 2.1, Theorem 1.5). || basis= None

E1-c104 L07 T | Does the competitive outcome maximize consumer surplus? || No. The competitive outcome maximizes total surplus (Lemma 1.4). Consumer surplus Q^2/(2b) is larger at Q = a, where the fee is zero, than at Q = d, because a > d (the misconception that the competitive outcome maximizes consumer surplus). || basis= None

E1-c105 L07 C | In Lab 1, do midpoint sums with 1, 2, 10 and 97 steps equal the exact integrals of P and P - c over [0, Q] for Q = d/2, 2d/3 and d? || Yes, exactly in all three cases, because both integrands are linear, so one step is already exact (Lab 1, Part A). || basis= Lab 1, Part A (exact arithmetic, three cases)

E1-c106 L08 None | What is a feasible allocation, and when is it interior? || An allocation (x, y) that A holds, with 0 <= x <= X and 0 <= y <= Y, while B holds (X - x, Y - y). It is interior when 0 < x < X and 0 < y < Y (Definition 1.1). || basis= None

E1-c107 L08 None | What is a Pareto improvement? || A feasible allocation z that gives each consumer at least its utility at w, and gives at least one consumer a strictly greater utility (Definition 1.2). || basis= None

E1-c108 L08 None | What is a Pareto efficient allocation? || A feasible allocation on which no feasible allocation is a Pareto improvement (Definition 1.3). || basis= None

E1-c109 L08 T | When are the two consumers' MRS equal at an interior allocation? || For an interior allocation, r_A = r_B if and only if yX = xY, with X > 0 and Y > 0 (Lemma 2.1). Here r_A = y/x and r_B = (Y - y)/(X - x). || basis= None

E1-c110 L08 T | Is an interior allocation off the diagonal yX = xY Pareto efficient? || Under the hypotheses X > 0, Y > 0, u_A = xy and u_B = (X - x)(Y - y): if an interior allocation has yX != xY, there is a feasible allocation that strictly raises both utilities, so it is not Pareto efficient (Proposition 2.2). || basis= None

E1-c111 L08 T | Is every feasible allocation on the diagonal Pareto efficient, including on the boundary? || Under the hypotheses X > 0, Y > 0, u_A = xy and u_B = (X - x)(Y - y): every feasible allocation with yX = xY is Pareto efficient, whether or not it is interior (Proposition 2.3). || basis= None

E1-c112 L08 T | Which interior allocations are Pareto efficient for product utilities? || Under the hypotheses X > 0, Y > 0, u_A = xy and u_B = (X - x)(Y - y): an interior allocation is Pareto efficient if and only if yX = xY. These are the points of the line y = (Y/X) x with 0 < x < X, the diagonal from the origin (0, 0) to the corner (X, Y) (Theorem 2.4). || basis= None

E1-c113 L08 T | Does Pareto efficiency rank efficient allocations? || No. For X = 8 and Y = 4, the allocations (6, 3) and (2, 1) are both efficient, with utilities (18, 2) and (2, 18). Neither is a Pareto improvement on the other (Example 2.5). || basis= None

E1-c114 L08 T | Does Theorem 2.4 cover boundary allocations? || No. It characterizes interior allocations only. Proposition 2.3 covers boundary allocations on the diagonal, and a boundary allocation off the diagonal is outside the theorem (Remark 2.6). || basis= None

E1-c115 L08 C | On the grid with X = 8 and Y = 4 in Lab 1, how many of the 45 feasible allocations are grid-efficient, and how many of those are interior? || 15 are grid-efficient: 13 interior and 2 on the boundary, (0, 0) and (8, 4) (Lab 1, Part A). || basis= Lab 1, Part A (one instance, exact arithmetic)

E1-c116 L08 T | Is a Pareto-efficient allocation the fair one? || No. Efficiency only says that no reshuffle helps one consumer without hurting the other. The allocations with utilities (18, 2) and (2, 18) are both efficient and differ in distribution (the misconception that a Pareto-efficient allocation is fair, Example 2.5). || basis= None

E1-c117 L08 T | Does a reshuffle that leaves nobody worse off count as a Pareto improvement? || No. Definition 1.2 also requires a strict gain for at least one consumer. A reshuffle that changes nothing is not a Pareto improvement (the misconception that a reshuffle that leaves nobody worse off is a Pareto improvement, Definition 1.2). || basis= None

E1-c118 L08 C | Is an allocation that no grid allocation improves necessarily efficient? || No. A grid test covers only the grid. The allocation (3, 1), with utilities 3 and 15, is improved by holdings A = (18/7, 6/5) and B = (38/7, 14/5), with utilities 108/35 and 76/5, both strictly higher (the misconception that an allocation no grid allocation improves is efficient, Lab 1 Part C). || basis= Lab 1, Part C (one instance, exact arithmetic)

E1-c119 L09 None | What is an externality? || An effect of one agent's activity on another agent's utility or profit that the market does not price. A negative externality lowers the other agent's welfare. When e > 0, the damage e is a negative externality (Definition 1.1). || basis= None

E1-c120 L09 T | What are the marginal social cost, the efficient quantity and the private quantity? || Marginal social cost is c + e. The efficient quantity q_s solves a - b q_s = c + e, so q_s = (a - c - e)/b. The private quantity q_p solves a - b q_p = c, so q_p = (a - c)/b (Definition 1.2). || basis= None

E1-c121 L09 T | By how much does the untaxed quantity exceed the efficient quantity? || By e/b units. The gap depends only on the damage e and the slope b, not on a or c. Under a > 0, b > 0, 0 <= c < a, e >= 0 and c + e < a, q_p - q_s = e/b (Proposition 1.3, and problem set item P1). || basis= None

E1-c122 L09 T | Does the efficient quantity maximize net surplus? || Yes. Under a > 0, b > 0 and c + e < a, V(q) = V(q_s) - (b/2)(q - q_s)^2 for every real q, where V(q) = aq - (b/2) q^2 - (c + e) q is the net surplus. So q_s is the unique maximizer (Proposition 1.4). || basis= None

E1-c123 L09 T | What is the efficiency loss of the untaxed market? || Under a > 0, b > 0, 0 <= c < a, e >= 0 and c + e < a: e^2/(2b), which equals V(q_s) - V(q_p), the shortfall of net surplus at the untaxed quantity (Corollary 1.5). || basis= None

E1-c124 L09 T | Which per-unit tax makes the market quantity efficient? || Under a > 0, b > 0, e >= 0 and c + e < a, among taxes t >= 0 only t = e, the Pigouvian tax, makes the market quantity efficient. The tax moves the untaxed outcome to q(t) = (a - c - t)/b, which equals q_s if and only if t = e. Its revenue e q_s is a transfer and is not part of V (Proposition 1.6). || basis= None

E1-c125 L09 None | What is a public good? || A good that is non-rival, so one consumer's use does not reduce the amount left for another, and non-excludable, so no consumer can be kept from consuming it. Every consumer then consumes the same quantity g. A consumer's willingness to pay at g is the price per unit at which that consumer would take exactly g units (Definition 2.1). || basis= None

E1-c126 L09 None | How is the demand of a public good aggregated, compared with a private good? || For a private good, individual quantities are summed at each price, a horizontal sum. For a public good, willingness to pay is summed at each quantity, the vertical sum (Definition 2.1). || basis= None

E1-c127 L09 T | What public-good quantity maximizes total surplus? || With two consumers whose willingness to pay w_1 and w_2 is continuous, non-negative and non-increasing, and a cost c_g > 0 per unit, let A = w_1 + w_2. If g* >= 0 and A(g*) = c_g, then g* maximizes S(g) = integral from 0 to g of A(s) ds - c_g g on [0, infinity) (Proposition 2.2). || basis= None

E1-c128 L09 T | With a negative externality, does the market produce too little? || No. The sign runs the other way: q_p - q_s = e/b > 0 when e > 0, so the untaxed market produces too much (the misconception that the market produces too little, Proposition 1.3). || basis= None

E1-c129 L09 C | In Lab 1 Part B, what quantity does the horizontal sum give at 400 cents, and how does its total surplus compare with the efficient quantity? || 7 units. Total surplus at 7 units is 2650 cents, below 2700 cents at the efficient 6 units (the misconception that the horizontal sum applies to a public good). || basis= Lab 1, Part B (exact arithmetic)

E1-c130 L09 T | In the Lab 1 market, what is the net-surplus loss of a 100-cent tax? || The tax leaves the market at (1000 - 200 - 100)/10 = 70 units, not 68. The net-surplus loss is (b/2)(70 - 68)^2 = 20 cents, with b = 10 (the misconception that any tax moves the market to the efficient quantity). || basis= None

E1-c131 L09 C | For inverse demand 1000 - 10q, private cost 200 and damage 120 cents per unit, what are the untaxed and efficient quantities in Lab 1, Part A? || 80 units untaxed and 68 units efficient, a gap of 12 units (Proposition 1.3, Lab 1, Part A). || basis= Lab 1, Part A (exact arithmetic, one instance)

E1-c132 L09 C | For the public good of Lab 1 with cost 400 cents per unit, which integer quantity from 0 to 10 maximises total surplus? || 6 units, where the vertical sum equals the cost (Proposition 2.2, Lab 1, Part B). || basis= Lab 1, Part B (exact arithmetic, one instance)

E1-c138 L08 T | What bound do the square roots of the two utilities satisfy at every feasible allocation? || Under the hypotheses X > 0, Y > 0, u_A = xy and u_B = (X - x)(Y - y): sqrt(u_A) + sqrt(u_B) <= sqrt(XY) for every feasible allocation, with equality exactly on the diagonal yX = xY (Proposition 2.3). || basis= None

E1-c149 L08 C | On the grid of totals X = 8 and Y = 4 in Lab 1, does each of the 10 off-diagonal interior grid-efficient allocations have an exact Pareto improvement? || Yes. Each has an exact allocation that strictly raises both utilities, with at least one amount that is not an integer, so the improvement lies off the grid (Proposition 2.2, Lab 1, Part C). || basis= Lab 1, Part C (one instance, exact arithmetic)

E1-c150 L09 C | For the Lab 1 market (inverse demand 1000 - 10q, private cost 200 and damage 120 cents per unit), which integer tax from 0 to 200 cents gives the efficient quantity of 68 units? || Only 120 cents, which is the damage (Proposition 1.6, Lab 1, Part A). || basis= Lab 1, Part A (exact arithmetic, one instance)

E1-c151 L09 C | For the Lab 1 public good (cost 400 cents per unit), what is total surplus at 6 units, in cents? || 2700 cents (Lab 1, Part B). || basis= Lab 1, Part B (exact arithmetic, one instance)

